1. Reference: Ma et al. (2003)
2. Instruction on how to run
3. Experiment design
4. Logic of the Design:
 |-- 4.1 IrisRecognition.py (main program): 
            The main program connects all the functions from the other scripts to perform iris recognition. It first loads the CASIA Iris Image Database and separates the images into 
        training and testing sets based on their sessions. In our dataset, there are 108 iris classes, with 3 training images and 4 testing images for each class. In the main program,
        we did not read in all images at once directly. Instead, we read them in as an array of file path, and then read every single image when needed, so we can reduce the memery usage.
            For each image, we first perform image quality assessment on both training and testing images. Althogh it is not required in our assignment, we tried to follow the instruction 
        in the paper to implement it. We calculated a quality descriptor to evaluate its quality based on frequency information. We then use the descriptors from the training images to 
        determine the quality thresholds and apply the same thresholds to both training and testing images. The result does not affect our training nor the testing sets. 
            Next, for each images, we perform image preprocessing and feature extraction using get_features function. In this function, each image is read in and processed sequentially. We 
        first use functions from IrisLocalization.py to identify the pupil and iris boundaries through iris localization. We then normaliza the iris image into a fixed size of 64*512 using
        function from IrisNormalization.py. After that, we use function from ImageEnhancement.py to enhance the normalized images. Finally, the enhanced image would be sent to the function 
        from FeatureExtraction.py to extract its features. For feature extraction, we use f=0.05 as the spatial frequency parameter. This value was selected during our initial implementation, 
        as it produced recognition accuracy above 80% (more explanation in 4.5 FeatureExtraction.py).
            To deal with possible iris rotation, we follow the rotation matching approach described by Ma et al. (2003) and generate seven different rotation versions for each training image
        using angles [-9, -6, -3, 0, 3, 6, 9]. The rotation is performed during iris normalization; therefore, we will need to run normalization, enhancement, and feature extraction for one
        image for 7 times if it is a training image. For testing images, they are only processed at 0 degrees.
            Then, we fitted a Fisher LDA model by using function from IrisMatching.py using training data (including all 7 angles). With the LDA mdoel, we transformed the original 1536 features 
        to 107 features for both training and the testing sets. 107 is the maximum number of LDA dimensions for our data, since our data contains only 108 iris classes. In the fit_fisher_lda 
        function, we first combine the original training features from all seven angles into one array, then fit and transform them using a single LDA model. After the transformation, we split 
        the reduced features back into seven arrays corresponding to the seven angles. With the returned LDA model, the testing features are then transformed in the main function.
            Finally, we used the reduced features for iris matching. We first created a figure to compare the performance of different LDA feature dimensions, using the test_dimensionality 
        function from IrisMatching.py. We selected [10, 20, 40, 60, 80, 90, 100, 107] dimensions for comparison. Since the LDA model has already been fitted with the maximum available dimensions, 
        we can directly select the first n components from the transformed features without fitting the model again. For each dimension, we perform iris matching using the three distance measures,
        which are L1, L2, and Cosine. Following the paper, we first calculated seven class centers for each class at each angles, and then calculate the distance between the testing features and 
        the centers. For the 7 centers in each class, the smallest distance is selected as the representing distance for the class. Among all the classes, the one with the smallest distance would
        be the predicted label. We repeated this process for all three methods, and calculated the CRR for the prediction, recoded the values and plot out the figure.
            After plotting out the figure, we found out the CRR seems to converge around 100 dimension, so we selected 100 dimensions for the final recognition and verification experiments.Then, 
        to create table 3 in Ma et al, we did iris matching on the original full 1536 features and compare how the reduction in dimension affect the performance. 
            Finally, we evaluate the iris recognition system in verification mode using the matching distances obtained from the selected 100-dimensional features using the evaluate_verification 
        function from PerformanceEvaluation.py. In this part, we evaluates whether an iris image matches a given class. We compare the testing images with all 108 iris classes and seprarate the 
        matching distance into genuine and impostor comparisons based on their true labels. Then, we calculate the False Match Rate (FMR) and False Non-Match Rate (FNMR) using different distance 
        thresholds. The result are used to generate Table 4 and plot the verification curves for L1, L2, and Cosine similarity measures.

 |-- 4.2 IrisLocalization.py
            This script follows the iris localization method in Ma et al. (2003, Section 3.2.1). It takes an eye image and returns two circles as (x, y, radius). One marks the edge of the pupil, and 
        the other marks where the iris meets the white of the eye. The circles are fitted separately because their centers can differ.

            Step 1. Find the rough pupil center. The pupil is usually the darkest part of the image. The code adds up the pixel values in each row and column. The row and column with the lowest totals 
        give the first guess for the pupil center.
            Step 2. Refine the center and estimate the radius. The code crops a 120 × 120 patch around that guess. It finds the most common gray level below 100 and adds 10 to set the cutoff for pupil 
        pixels. A 5 × 5 filter removes thin dark shapes such as eyelashes. The average position of the remaining pixels gives the new center, and their total area gives an estimate of the radius. 
        The code repeats this step twice.
            Step 3. Fit both circles. Canny detects edges, and the circular Hough transform picks the circle with the strongest support from those edges. The pupil search stays close to the estimates 
        from step 2. The outer iris search allows radii from 90 to 135 pixels and keeps the center close to the pupil. It also uses lower Canny thresholds to pick up the fainter outer edge. We tuned 
        these settings by looking at the circles drawn on the dataset images and checking whether images of the same eye had similar iris radii.

            The script also includes the image quality check from Section 3.1. It uses the rough pupil location to take two 64 × 64 patches, one on each side of the pupil, where the iris is less likely 
        to be covered by eyelids. For each patch, a Fourier transform measures the strength of low, middle, and high frequencies (F1, F2, and F3). The code calculates the total strength F1 + F2 + F3 and
        the middle-frequency ratio F2 / (F1 + F3), then averages the results from both patches.

 |-- 4.3 IrisNormalization.py
            This script follows the iris normalization method in Ma et al. (2003, Section 3.2.2). The iris can look bigger or smaller depending on the camera distance and how much the pupil opens. 
        To make images easier to compare, the script unwraps the ring-shaped iris into a rectangle with 64 rows and 512 columns.
            The columns go once around the iris counterclockwise. At each angle, the code finds one point on the pupil boundary and another on the outer iris boundary. It then samples along the line 
        between those two points. The first row starts at the pupil, and each row after that moves one step toward the outer edge. This follows Equation (2) and works even when the two circles have 
        different centers.
            When a sample falls between pixels, OpenCV blends the four neighboring pixel values using bilinear interpolation. It uses zero for pixels beyond the image border.
            The "init_angle_deg" setting changes where the unwrapping starts, which shifts the iris pattern across the columns. For rotation matching, we can reuse the same two boundary circles and 
        unwrap each training image at seven starting angles (−9, −6, −3, 0, 3, 6, and 9 degrees).

 |-- 4.4 ImageEnhancement.py:
            This script implements the image enhancement method proposed by Ma et al. (2003), Section 3.2.3. It takes a normalized iris image with shape (64, 512) as input, estimates
         and compensates for background illumation, and then applies local histogram equalization to improve the contrast of the iris textures. One thing that we found worth noticed
         is that the authors often do operation or feature extractions within a small block of image (locally) so the difference over the whole image would still remain.
            The first step is to estimate the background illumination of the normalized image. We divided the imiage into many 16*16 small blocks and calculate tge average pixel 
        intensity of each block. Since the image size is 64*512, the result background illumination map would be 4*32. THen, we need to substract the background illumation from the 
        original normalized image, so we extend the 4*32 map to 64*512 using the bicubic interpolation mentioned in Ma et a. (2003), which estimates intermediate values to create a
        smoother background illumation map. After substracting the estimated background illumation from the original normalized image, it is now ready for histogram equalization.
            To do histogram equalization, we divided the corrected image into many 32*32 small blocks and apply histogram equalization independently to each block. By doing this,
        the local contrast would be improved and the iris textures would be more visible. The final enhanced images would remain the shape of 64*512.

 |-- 4.5 FeatureExtraction.py:
            This script implements the feature extraction method proposed by Ma et al. (2003), Section 3.3. It takes a normalized and enhanced iris image with shape (64, 512) as input
        and extracts 1536 features from each image.
            First, we selected only the upper 48 rows of the enhanced image as our Range of Interest (ROI), so the shape of the image would become 48*512. We are doing this because 
        according to Ma et al. (2003), the iris region closer to the pupil contains more useful texture information and is less likely to be affected by eyelids and eyelashes.  
            Next, we construct two spatial filters using Equation (3) from Ma et al.To construct each spatial filter kernel, we first determine its size based on the larger value between delta_x 
        and delta_y. We used (6 * maximum delta vlaue + 1) as the kernel size to make sure it has a center (0,0) and covers most of the Gaussian envelope (3-sigma rule). Then, the kernels are 
        plugged into the formula provided in Ma et al. to create spatial filters. The filters use a Gaussian envelope and a radial cosine function to capture local iris texture information.
        The two sets of parameters delta_x and delta_y given in the paper are (3, 1.5) and (4.5, 1.5), where they control the spread of Gaussian envelope along the horizontal and vertical
        directions. The parameter f controls the spatial frequency of the cosine function. A larger f captures high-frequency patterns better, and a lower f captures lower ones better. The 
        value f is not mentioned in Ma et al., so we looks up for f that are often used in Gabor filters, and decided to try out values between 0.05 and 0.4. We first tried out 0.05, and the 
        results are very good, so we did not move on to trying other values. After having a filter, we apply them to the ROI using OpenC's filter2D() function. It is noticable that after 
        applying the filters, the ROI now may contains numbers that are out of the range of [0,255], and could have decimal numbers. The shape remains to be 48*512. 
            For each filtered ROI, we divided the image into many 8*8 small blocks to extract 2 statistical features from each block. Since the shape of ROI is 48*512, we have 384*2 features for
        each filtered ROI. In Ma et al., the 2 features are m and sigma. THe first feature m is the mean absolute filter response; this represents the average response strength of the local 
        iris texture. The second feature sigme is the mean absolute deviation of the filter response magnitudes from their mean. This measures how much the response magnitudes vary within the 
        block. Finally, we would get 768 features for each filtered ROI. As we have 2 filtered ROI, we would have 1536 features for image.

 |-- 4.6 IrisMatching.py
 |-- 4.7 PerformanceEvaluation.py

5. Result: 
   With our Effect of LDA Dimensions on Iris Recognition plot (Fig. 10 in the paper), we found that the performance of the matching significantly improved as the dimension grows
   from 20 to 40. Then, the growing speeds started to decrease, finally seems to converge at aobut 100-dimension. We also noticed that the cosine matching method seems to perform
   the best among the three, and then L2, and then L1. Since it seems to converge at around 100-dimension, we selected 100 as our reduced LDA dimension to do comparison and 
   verifications. For 100-dimension reduced features, the Correct Recognition Rate (CRR) for L1, L2, and Cosine methods are 85.88%, 88.19%, and 91,20% correspondingly. 

   Belows are the outputed Table 3 and Table 4 for our algorithm:

    Table 3: Recognition Results
    Method       Original (%)    Reduced (%)    
    L1           93.98           85.88          
    L2           87.50           88.19          
    Cosine       87.96           91.20       

    From Table 3, we noticed that the L1 matching method using all 1536 original features achieved the highest CRR of 93.98% among the six results. This somewhat matches our 
    expectation that having more features could provide more information for recognition. However, it is also surprising because L1 generally performed worse than the other 
    two methods in our LDA dimension comparison. One possible explanation is that L1 is more sensitive to the information lost during dimensionality reduction.
    On the other hand, L2 and Cosine performed better with the reduced features than with the original features. This suggests that Fisher LDA may help these two methods by 
    removing redundant information and improving the separation between different iris classes. In particular, Cosine achieved a CRR of 91.20% using only 100 features, 
    compared to 87.96% using all 1536 features.
    Overall, although dimensionality reduction does not improve the performance of every matching method, it allows us to significantly reduce the number of features while 
    maintaining relatively high recognition accuracy. This also suggests that the effectiveness of dimensionality reduction depends on the similarity measure being used.
    
