6. Difference from the paper:
   For this project, we mostly followed the instruction in the paper, unless it is not stated or stated in our assignment requriement. 
   The following are some differences:
        1. Dataset: Ma et al. uses a larger CASIA Iris Database with 306 iris classes and 2255 selected images. This results in out maximum 
                    reduced feature dimension being 107 instead of 305.
        2. Image Quality Assessment: Although we did verify the image quality, because of the assignment requirement, we did not remove the 
                                     images that have low quality.
        3. Image Enhancement: We performed min-max normalization step after background subtraction to rescale the pixel values into the range [0,255].
                              This is not stated in the paper, but this step is necessary in order to use OpenCV's histogram equalization.
        4. Feature Extraction: f and kernel size are not stated in the paper, so we did research and selected some values that seem reasonable. 
        5. Testing Different Numbers of LDA Dimensions: The paper did not states what values they tested out for, but their plot seems to be oughly every
                                                        20 dimension, so we also did the similar thing, with some additional dimensionalities to test out 
                                                        since our maximum dimension is smaller.
        6. Verification: We plotted the FMR and FNMR curves for L1, L2, and Cosine. Unlike the original paper, we did not implement additional recognition 
                         methods for comparison or calculate confidence intervals using Bootstrap, as these were not required by our assignment.

7. Limitations and Plausible improvement
        All three correct recognition rates (CRRs) were above 80%, meeting the assignment requirement. The paper reported 99.43% on a larger dataset after selecting a 
    frame from each image sequence. The two results come from different evaluation setups.
        1. The iris boundary can be misplaced. Dark eyelashes or image borders can throw off the first pupil estimate. Eyelid edges can also pull the outer circle away 
    from the actual iris boundary. In our check of 756 images, two pupil centers were off by more than 100 pixels, and about 65 outer circles hit a radius limit. One plausible
    solution is by searching only the center of the image for the pupil and keeping side edges where the dark iris meets the brighter white of the eye.
        2. Eyelids and eyelashes can hide iris texture. Both methods use the 48 rows closest to the pupil because this area is usually less covered. In narrow eyes, eyelids and 
    eyelashes can still reach those rows. One solution is to detect and mask the covered pixels before calculating texture features.
        3. Some settings were chosen by hand. The paper gives the filter formula and Gaussian settings, but leaves out details such as the exact filter frequency, kernel size, 
    and localization settings. We chose values through trial and error. A better approach would tune them on a validation split drawn from the training images and reserve the 
    test set for final evaluation.
        4. LDA may overfit. We use three original training images per eye. Rotated copies help with alignment, but they add little independent information for learning from 1,536 features. 
    Our L1 recognition rate fell from 93.98% to 85.88% after LDA. With 108 iris classes, our LDA can produce at most 107 features. The paper used 200 features with 306 classes. One better way
    might be testing PCA before LDA or try regularized LDA.
        5. Our verification results are weaker.At a false match rate of 0.1%, our best false nonmatch rate was 14.815% using cosine similarity. The paper reported 0.97%. We could 
    test whether better localization reduces missed matches, use a log scale to make the low-FMR region easier to read, and bootstrap by iris class to report confidence intervals.